1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,852
This is a real, three-dimensional model of a human

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:06,477
heart, built from an open anatomical atlas.

3
00:00:06,777 --> 00:00:09,773
Let's explore how it works, from the outside in.

4
00:00:11,573 --> 00:00:13,221
At the top are the great vessels.

5
00:00:13,521 --> 00:00:16,273
The aorta carries oxygen-rich blood out to the body.

6
00:00:16,573 --> 00:00:19,940
The pulmonary trunk carries oxygen-poor blood to the lungs.

7
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:24,176
And the superior vena cava brings blood back from the upper body.

8
00:00:24,476 --> 00:00:27,250
The coronary arteries, shown in red, wrap around

9
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,793
the surface to feed the heart muscle itself.

10
00:00:31,593 --> 00:00:33,729
Now let's make the muscle transparent.

11
00:00:34,029 --> 00:00:35,666
Inside are four chambers.

12
00:00:35,966 --> 00:00:38,543
On the heart's right side, the right atrium

13
00:00:38,543 --> 00:00:41,121
and right ventricle hold oxygen-poor blood.

14
00:00:41,421 --> 00:00:46,158
On its left side, the left atrium and left ventricle hold oxygen-rich blood.

15
00:00:47,958 --> 00:00:50,814
Four valves keep the blood moving in one direction.

16
00:00:51,114 --> 00:00:55,537
Seen from above, the tricuspid and mitral valves open into the ventricles.

17
00:00:55,837 --> 00:00:59,355
The pulmonary and aortic valves open into the great arteries.

18
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:02,583
Now, follow the blood.

19
00:01:02,883 --> 00:01:07,380
Oxygen-poor blood from the body fills the right atrium, passes the tricuspid valve

20
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:12,043
into the right ventricle, and is pumped through the pulmonary valve toward the lungs.

21
00:01:12,343 --> 00:01:16,048
Oxygen-rich blood returns from the lungs through the pulmonary veins

22
00:01:16,048 --> 00:01:19,644
into the left atrium, flows through the mitral valve into the left

23
00:01:19,644 --> 00:01:23,187
ventricle, and is pumped through the aortic valve into the aorta.

24
00:01:25,487 --> 00:01:29,934
With each beat, the atria contract first, topping up the ventricles.

25
00:01:30,234 --> 00:01:33,236
Then the ventricles contract, and the valves close

26
00:01:33,236 --> 00:01:35,818
in turn, making the familiar lub-dub sound.

27
00:01:40,618 --> 00:01:42,882
Slicing through the heart reveals its walls.

28
00:01:43,182 --> 00:01:46,871
The left ventricle's wall is about ten millimetres thick, far thicker

29
00:01:46,871 --> 00:01:50,508
than the right's, because it must pump blood around the entire body.

30
00:01:52,808 --> 00:01:55,319
Everything in this video, from the anatomy

31
00:01:55,319 --> 00:01:57,951
model to the voice, is free and open source.

